<%# THE HERO CARD — Home's first three answers (answers-first spec §§2-3).
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). It replaces `home/_standing.html.erb`.

    ONE CARD IN EVERY STATE, and that is the design rather than a simplification of it. The band
    this replaced opened with `if presenter.covered?` and had two headlines, two sublines and a
    reconciliation clause that had to be reachable from both — and it still got the empty-budget
    user wrong, printing "You're covered this period" over a $100 deficit it called unclaimed money.
    There is no branch here that decides WHAT to say: every state says "In Checking", "Free to
    spend" and where the period is, and a bad state is a red figure with a sentence, not a different
    card. See spec §2, which rules the covered/uncovered question closed.

    THE WORDS ARE THE USER'S (spec §3). In checking, free, set aside, spoken for. "Available" is the
    mechanic's term and survives on Distribute and Budget; "buffer" and "unclaimed" are dead. %>
<div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-4 sm:p-6" data-hero>
  <dl class="space-y-4">
    <%# THE NUMBER THE BANK APP SHOWS. `#in_checking` is `AccountLedger#pot` — main's balance, not
        the user's money everywhere — because that is the figure they can check against their phone.
        RED WHEN IT IS BELOW ZERO (spec §2): a physical overdraft is the loudest fact on the screen,
        and unlike every purpose-ledger figure it is money that has already gone. %>
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
      <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-600">In Checking</dt>
      <dd class="text-2xl font-semibold tabular-nums <%= presenter.in_checking.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>"
          data-in-checking>
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.in_checking) %>
      </dd>
    </div>

    <%# ONE PLAIN SENTENCE, modelled on the overdraft strip the standing band ended with. What
        changes is the last clause: that strip said "none of the figures above count it", which was
        true of a band whose figures were both purpose-ledger ones and is FALSE here — the figure it
        sits under is the overdraft. The strip's own copy survives verbatim below, for the accounts
        it is still exactly true of.

        THE FACT ALONE, AND NO CONSEQUENCE (fix round 1 — MED-1). This ended "— nothing is free
        until money comes in", which is a lie for the user whose money is simply in the wrong
        account: $1,000 of income with $1,200 walked over to savings puts the pot at -$200 with
        $1,000 unspoken for, and what that user needs is a transfer, not income. Being spent past
        zero is true in BOTH negative-pot states; which of the two it is belongs to the subline,
        which is the one place on this card that knows. %>
    <% if presenter.in_checking.negative? %>
      <p class="-mt-2 text-sm text-status-danger" data-checking-overdrawn>
        Your checking account is already spent past zero.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE ONE DERIVED NUMBER: `min(pot, available − remaining_plan)` (spec §3). Larger than the
        figure above because it is the question the user cannot answer from their bank app — the
        pot they can. Never clamped: a negative free is rendered as a negative. %>
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
      <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-600">Free to spend</dt>
      <dd class="text-3xl font-semibold tabular-nums <%= presenter.free_to_spend.negative? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>"
          data-free-to-spend>
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.free_to_spend) %>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <%# WHERE THE DIFFERENCE WENT, in three words the user already owns — and FIVE arms, because
      every sentence here asserts a CAUSE and each one has to be gated on a predicate that
      ESTABLISHES that cause (FINAL review — M-1). The signs of the two figures say which way the
      arithmetic went and never why: `available − pot` is `moves out + swept − Σ holdings`, so a
      cap-bound card gated on the signs alone told a SINGLE-account user their money was "sitting
      outside checking" when what had actually happened was a category being overspent — a sentence
      about savings above an accounts line with nothing in it.

      THE ARM TABLE IS IN THE PRESENTER, beside the four predicates these branches ask, written as
      signs × causes. In order, the arms are:

        the plan outruns the money   → and something IS set aside or spoken for, or (§10.7 #3) the
                                       account is a pure overspend and neither noun is true of it.
        free is negative, plan does  → and the money really is in other accounts, or there is no
        not outrun (the cap bound      other account holding anything — in which case a negative pot
        on a negative pot)             IS the whole of the user's cash and the plain sentence is the
                                       true one. Its overdraft is already stated one line up.
        free is fine, some of the    → "the rest is set aside or spoken for".
        pot is claimed
        free is fine, none of it is  → "none of it is set aside or spoken for", and the parked
        claimed                        clause only where a second account actually holds money.
                                       This is L-4's fresh signup, whose two figures are the same
                                       number because nothing is funded — the card used to describe
                                       a "rest" of $0.00 to them.

      EVERY GATE IS THE PRESENTER'S OWN PREDICATE rather than a comparison spelled here. A view
      re-deriving which side of the `min` won, or re-deriving whether another account holds money,
      could print a sentence about arithmetic the figure beside it did not do. %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600" data-free-subline>
    <% if presenter.plan_outruns_the_money? %>
      <% if presenter.anything_set_aside_or_spoken_for? %>
        More is set aside or spoken for than you have. Anything you spend now takes you further under.
      <% else %>
        You have spent past what you had. Anything you spend now takes you further under.
      <% end %>
    <% elsif presenter.free_to_spend.negative? %>
      <%# NEITHER HALF ASSERTS AN ACCOUNT BY NAME, deliberately: `available` spans every account the
          user has, so "it is in savings" is a guess this card cannot make. What it can say is that
          the money is not spoken for and not here, which is the whole of what the cap means. %>
      <% if presenter.money_parked_elsewhere? %>
        The money that isn't spoken for is sitting outside checking — nothing here is free until some
        of it moves in.
      <% else %>
        Nothing here is free until money comes in.
      <% end %>
    <% elsif presenter.rest_in_checking? %>
      the rest is set aside or spoken for.
    <% else %>
      none of it is set aside or spoken for<% if presenter.free_cap_bound? && presenter.money_parked_elsewhere? %> —
      more is parked in other accounts<% end %>.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <%# WHERE WE ARE IN THE PERIOD (spec §2), as a bar rather than a date range: "Aug 14 – Aug 27" is
      a fact the reader has to do arithmetic on, and "7 days left" is the answer they wanted.

      SILENT FOR AN UNDECLARED USER — `#period_progress` is nil, the same gate `#period_range` has
      always applied. Inventing a calendar-month boundary nobody set would be a lie beside two
      figures that are still true.

      The track/fill pair is `categories/_partials/_category_card`'s, so the app's two bars are one
      shape; `role="progressbar"` because a bar with no accessible value is decoration to a screen
      reader, and the days-left sentence beside it is the value in words either way. %>
  <% if progress = presenter.period_progress %>
    <div class="mt-5 border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 text-xs text-gray-500" data-period-range>
        <span><%= progress.first.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
        <span><%= progress.last.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden"
           role="progressbar"
           aria-valuenow="<%= progress.percent %>"
           aria-valuemin="0"
           aria-valuemax="100"
           aria-label="Day <%= progress.day %> of <%= progress.days %> in this period"
           data-period-progress="<%= progress.percent %>">
        <div class="h-1.5 rounded-full bg-brand-dark" style="width: <%= progress.percent %>%;"></div>
      </div>
      <%# HOOKED, because the SAME sentence is printed by `_this_period`'s heading: a page-wide
          `have_content("7 days left")` in hero_spec passes off the section below without the card
          rendering anything at all. %>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-gray-500" data-period-days-left>
        <%= pluralize(progress.days_left, "day") %> left
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ** TWO THINGS THAT WERE PARKED HERE AND ARE NOW GONE (answers-first Task 2). ** Task 1 kept
      both on this card because the trouble strip did not exist yet, and named them as a carry:

        THE NON-MAIN OVERDRAFT STRIP — `Ally is overdrawn $200.00 — already spent, and none of the
          figures above count it`. It is spec §5's "physical overdraft" trigger, so it belongs on
          the strip, and its copy is verbatim there. Main's own overdraft never moved: it IS the red
          figure at the top of this card, with the sentence above.
        §9'S PERMANENT SACRIFICE BUTTON — "Your budget doesn't fit your income". A strip that renders
          only when something is true is the right home for a verdict that is only rendered when it
          is true, and it is the one kind of trouble no reallocation can fix.

      Said here rather than deleted silently, because a card that used to carry two features and now
      carries none of them is exactly where a later reader would assume they were dropped. %>
</div>
